WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
Functions are a way to help us organize our code in a way that is reusable.

00:06.000 --> 00:08.000
It lets us accept our humans.

00:08.000 --> 00:14.000
Like we saw earlier on in the class that also lets us provide defaults we're needed.

00:14.000 --> 00:19.000
And loops let us take actions on collections of items that we've built up,

00:19.000 --> 00:21.000
our lists, our dictionaries, or tuples.

00:21.000 --> 00:27.000
And then we can use Boolean logic to control the flow of the program.

00:28.000 --> 00:32.000
Now, we've been working in the Rebels so far.

00:32.000 --> 00:36.000
It's time to move on to working in Python files.

00:36.000 --> 00:41.000
We want to be able to save and reuse and run again.

00:41.000 --> 00:49.000
So if you need to create a Python file, it ends in a .py extension.

00:49.000 --> 00:53.000
There are some naming conventions that you can find in Pepe 8 guidelines.

00:53.000 --> 00:58.000
But for the most part, Python file names should be all in lower case.

00:58.000 --> 01:00.000
They should be separated.

01:00.000 --> 01:03.000
The word should be separated with underscores.

01:03.000 --> 01:06.000
Found names should be as short as possible.

01:06.000 --> 01:09.000
And they should end in .py.

01:09.000 --> 01:13.000
We're going to talk about modules a little bit later.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
But you don't want to, it's called smurf name, your files.

01:17.000 --> 01:21.000
So you don't want to say my underscore lib APIs.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
My underscore lib helpers.

01:24.000 --> 01:26.000
My underscore lib utilities.

01:26.000 --> 01:31.000
Instead, you would put all of those in a folder called my lib.

01:31.000 --> 01:34.000
And just name them the simple file names.

01:34.000 --> 01:36.000
Just APIs.

01:36.000 --> 01:38.000
Just exceptions.

01:38.000 --> 01:40.000
Just blog.py.

01:40.000 --> 01:43.000
Here are some bad example file names.

01:43.000 --> 01:45.000
Don't name your Python files and all caps.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
Don't use camel case.

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
So capitalizing the first letter of each word.

01:51.000 --> 01:57.000
That is the best practice or standard from a different programming language.

01:57.000 --> 02:05.000
And don't use smurfy really underscore long descriptive project file name.py.

02:05.000 --> 02:13.000
You might see .py files in your directory as you start running your Python projects.

02:13.000 --> 02:19.000
Those are compiled intermediary Python files.

02:19.000 --> 02:21.000
They are for optimization.

02:21.000 --> 02:23.000
You don't have to worry about them.

02:23.000 --> 02:29.000
But you do need to be careful about not committing them to get.

02:29.000 --> 02:33.000
So those PIC files are just, they don't matter.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
They don't need to be included with your program.

02:36.000 --> 02:41.000
And sometimes, stale versions of these files can cause problems.

02:41.000 --> 02:47.000
For example, if you're checking out a different branch and get and your PIC files don't really match.

02:47.000 --> 02:54.000
So if you're running into weird problems when working with Python files,

02:54.000 --> 03:03.000
you can use this command on Mac or Linux to find all of those PIC files and just blow them away all at once.

03:03.000 --> 03:10.000
We already saw how to run Python programs from VS code and how to create new Python files.

03:10.000 --> 03:21.000
If you want to run that same file from your terminal, you just need to make sure that you have your virtual environment activated.

03:21.000 --> 03:25.000
And let's see what I have in this file.

03:25.000 --> 03:31.000
So again, when your virtual environment is activated, you can just use Python.

03:31.000 --> 03:34.000
Not Python 3 or Python 3.9.

03:34.000 --> 03:39.000
Python is the executable in your virtual environment that you know maps to the right place.

03:39.000 --> 03:46.000
And then you would just pass in the file name.

03:46.000 --> 03:50.000
Or in VS code.

03:50.000 --> 03:54.000
We would want to make sure to exit our REPL.

03:54.000 --> 03:58.000
And then do run Python file in terminal.

03:58.000 --> 04:00.000
And that will go ahead and do the same thing.

04:00.000 --> 04:05.000
So those are the different options.

04:05.000 --> 04:11.000
Once we move on to files, remember earlier I said that the REPL when you type a variable or string.

04:11.000 --> 04:14.000
It'll just show you the value right then and there.

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
That functionality does not exist in files.

04:17.000 --> 04:23.000
So if you want to see the output in Python files, you're going to need to start putting print statements in them.

04:23.000 --> 04:30.000
So make sure that you print the values of your strings if you want to see them.

04:30.000 --> 04:33.000
If not, that's totally fine as well.

04:33.000 --> 04:39.000
You can have print statements in while you debug and then comment them out once your program is ready.

04:39.000 --> 04:49.000
Or once you start writing more advanced programs, you might use the logger module, which lets you configure how output from your Python program is presented.

04:49.000 --> 04:52.000
That is outside of the scope of the course.

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
So I won't be covering it today.

04:55.000 --> 05:08.000
So if you're going to be sharing your code with a team or you plan on sharing it or you're working in production, you generally don't want to leave print statements littered in your code.

05:08.000 --> 05:11.000
Those are for your own debugging.

05:11.000 --> 05:16.000
So something to be mindful of.

05:17.000 --> 05:25.000
Output formatting tips remember earlier showed you the backslash n signified in new line backslash t is for tab.

05:25.000 --> 05:34.000
You can use the special control characters for your advantage when you're printing stuff out and you want to make sure that it is readable.

05:34.000 --> 05:41.000
You can also pretty print with something called the pretty print module.

05:41.000 --> 05:47.000
And I want to show that too quickly because I find it super useful.

05:47.000 --> 05:54.000
So opening up my repo here.

05:54.000 --> 05:58.000
I'm going to create a long list.

05:58.000 --> 06:02.000
If I tried to print this, it's not very readable.

06:02.000 --> 06:05.000
Pretty print is included in the Python standard library.

06:05.000 --> 06:09.000
So I can say from paperint import paperint.

06:09.000 --> 06:16.000
And that will let me access this module.

06:16.000 --> 06:22.000
Because paperint is not one of those built in functions that we saw.

06:22.000 --> 06:23.000
It's not Len.

06:23.000 --> 06:26.000
It's not print where we can just use it without importing it.

06:26.000 --> 06:28.000
This is kind of a special case.

06:28.000 --> 06:38.000
And once you have imported it, we can pass in the long list here and it will go ahead and print it out with line breaks.

06:38.000 --> 06:45.000
So if you're looking at complex code, this is a great tool to use to make it more human readable.

